  "summary": "The candidate has real UI implementation mass and covers the main workspace layout, required-field gating, and exception affordance, but it is not yet hardening-ready. One acceptance point is only partially met because operator-visible copy is not fully Italian, the error-state branch is effectively broken by control-flow order, and tests lean heavily on injected fixtures instead of proving the real query/error path.",
  "scope_fidelity": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "Core REQ-008 behavior is present: single-screen layout, preview pane, status badge, extracted vs editable values, confidence/provenance display, required-field highlighting, and exception retry affordance.",
      "The validation action is correctly kept as a seam for REQ-009 rather than falsely implemented here.",
      "Operator-visible localization is incomplete: labels such as \"Confidence\" and \"Correlation ID\" remain English-facing, and technical field keys are surfaced directly.",
      "Exception handling for 401/403/404 is described and coded, but the current screen control flow returns the loading shell whenever data is absent, so query failures do not reliably reach the dedicated error UI."
    ]
  },
  "source_substance": {
    "status": "good_with_gaps",
    "notes": [
      "This is not skeletal. The candidate contains a substantial primary component, typed DTOs, a runtime adapter, a Zustand store, a query hook, and a route page.",
      "The UI logic for required-field completion and state-based action availability is real and localized enough to demonstrate the intended workflow shape.",
      "The source is weaker around promotable integration seams: fetch/error handling is slice-local instead of aligned with the planned shared frontend API layer, and the current error branch logic undermines claimed unauthorized/forbidden/not-found behavior.",
      "The component is large and monolithic; promotion is possible, but hardening should reduce risk around state and error behavior before considering it stable."
    ]
  },
  "test_substance": {
    "status": "weak",
    "notes": [
      "Tests do prove some real UI behavior: required fields block completion, extracted and editable panels are distinct, and retry affordance appears in exception state.",
      "Most tests bypass the real data-loading path by supplying initialDocument fixtures, so they do not validate fetch, query lifecycle, or runtime error handling.",
      "There is no direct test for 401/403/404 rendering, no proof that the current bugged error branch is caught, and no meaningful dual-profile coverage beyond command packaging.",
      "Browser smoke is component-level mount coverage, not end-to-end route/integration evidence."
    ]
  },
  "docs_code_coherence": {
    "status": "mixed",
    "notes": [
      "README, KIT, HOWTO, and LTC are internally organized and generally describe the emitted files accurately.",
      "Docs claim explicit handling of 401/403/404 states, but the current component flow makes those states unreliable because `!data` returns the loading UI before the error branches.",
      "Docs present operator-facing Italian UX as a completed outcome, but some visible labels remain English.",
      "CI and HOWTO commands are staging-aware and operationally plausible, though they depend on canonical repo tooling that is not demonstrated here."
    ]
  },
  "promotion_fit": {
    "status": "partial",
    "notes": [
      "The candidate stays within the planned `frontend.app.validation.workspace` family and is localized enough to promote into canonical `src/` with limited path churn.",
      "It does not invent unnecessary top-level roots, which is good for promotion safety.",
      "Promotion fit is reduced by slice-local runtime/fetch handling instead of use of the planned shared frontend API conventions, and by the unproven error path.",
      "The staging package is reviewable, but it still needs targeted hardening before it is a credible promotion candidate."
    ]
  },
  "shared_common_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable_with_missed_alignment",
    "notes": [
      "The candidate does not introduce unjustified shared/common extraction.",
      "That restraint is preferable to premature abstraction.",
      "However, the plan expected alignment with shared frontend API/UI conventions; the current slice keeps its own fetch/error wrapper rather than showing reuse of those seams."
    ]
  },
  "dual_mode_assessment": {
    "status": "acceptable",
    "notes": [
      "Local and AWS modes preserve the same page shape, DTO contract, and business UI behavior.",
      "Runtime differences are limited to configuration of the backend base URL, which is the right architectural direction for a promotable frontend slice.",
      "Dual-mode integrity is only lightly evidenced because tests do not meaningfully exercise both profiles."
    ]
  },
  "blockers": [
    "Error-state handling is not trustworthy: when query data is absent the screen returns the loading shell before the dedicated 401/403/404 branches, so documented failure behavior is effectively broken.",
    "Operator-visible localization is incomplete, which leaves REQ-008 acceptance only partially satisfied.",
    "Tests do not cover the real query/error path and therefore miss the current control-flow defect."
  ],
  "hardening_targets": [
    "Harden screen state flow so loading, success, and fetch-error states are mutually correct and 401/403/404 rendering is actually reachable.",
    "Complete operator-facing Italian localization for all visible labels and avoid surfacing raw technical field identifiers where operator labels should suffice.",
    "Add tests for real query execution and API error states instead of relying mainly on `initialDocument` fixtures.",
    "Add focused tests for state-dependent actions, including validation callback invocation after required fields are completed and exception screens where retry is not allowed.",
    "Align the slice runtime/API seam more closely with the planned shared frontend API conventions to reduce promotion friction."
  ]
}
